<include>

  <!--
    Coral X push wake (RFC 8599 / ADR-004) - github.com/Rahulcse79/coralx-push-sender.

    Matches ANY number that is a user in this server's directory, so it needs no editing
    per deployment: no extension range, no IP address. $${domain} is the server's own SIP
    domain from vars.xml, and user_exists is answered by the directory.

    Media is BYPASSED for every handset-to-handset call (bypass_media=true): the SDP
    crosses FreeSWITCH untouched and the two handsets negotiate the codec between
    themselves, with RTP flowing phone-to-phone. This is what lets two Coral X handsets
    talk Lyra (ADR-008) - a codec FreeSWITCH does not know and would otherwise strip from
    the offer, which ends the call as 488 INCOMPATIBLE_DESTINATION. The price is that
    FreeSWITCH is not in the media path, so handsets on two different NATs cannot reach
    each other; on such a deployment use proxy_media=true instead (RTP relayed, still not
    decoded, so Lyra still passes). Calls to the test applications (9196-9199, 3000) are
    not affected - they are separate extensions and need FreeSWITCH's own media.

    Why one bridge is not enough: a Coral X handset that is asleep, dozing or swiped
    from Recents still has a registration row here, but nothing behind it - the INVITE
    to that contact gets no 100 Trying and dies as ORIGINATOR_CANCEL after call_timeout.
    "Registered" is not "reachable". So:

      1. announce the call (the push sender hears this event and sends the FCM wake
         immediately, in parallel with step 2);
      2. try the contact we have, but give it only progress_timeout seconds to say
         100/180 - an app that is awake answers well inside that, a dead socket never
         will - and only the "unreachable" causes fall through (a 486 from a live
         callee still reaches the caller as a 486);
      3. ring the caller and park. The sender un-parks the call into coralx-resume the
         moment the device's fresh REGISTER lands, or into coralx-timeout when it does
         not (12 s). sched_transfer is the belt and braces for a sender that is down:
         15 s later the call goes to coralx-resume on its own, which is exactly the
         single direct bridge this rule replaces.

    Put per-user special routing (call forwarding, IVRs) in a file that sorts BEFORE
    this one, or in the body of default.xml; the first matching extension wins. Note
    that an extension in the body of default.xml matches BEFORE anything in this
    directory, and that a nested <condition> on a variable that is only set at execute
    time (e.g. ${cfwd_to}) is evaluated at parse time, where it is empty - the actions
    are queued anyway and the extension does not stop the search, so the next matching
    rules run too. That is how the April agent_1003/agent_1004 rules used to swallow
    calls to 1003 and 1004 before this one; they were removed on 2026-09-13.
  -->
  <extension name="coralx-push-wake">
    <condition field="${user_exists(id ${destination_number} $${domain})}" expression="^true$">
      <action application="set" data="hangup_after_bridge=true"/>
      <action application="set" data="call_timeout=30"/>
      <action application="set" data="bypass_media=true"/>

      <!-- 1. Announce. Fired before the first attempt so the wake-up is already in
              flight while the direct INVITE is finding out the socket is dead.
              Event-Name=CUSTOM is required: without it the event app fires a
              CHANNEL_APPLICATION event, which an ESL `event CUSTOM coralx::push_wake`
              subscription never sees. -->
      <action application="event" data="Event-Name=CUSTOM,Event-Subclass=coralx::push_wake,Push-Ext=${destination_number},Push-Caller=${caller_id_number}"/>

      <!-- 2. The direct attempt: 3 s for a provisional response, and fall through only
              on the causes that mean "nobody is there", never on a rejection. -->
      <action application="set" data="progress_timeout=3"/>
      <action application="set" data="continue_on_fail=PROGRESS_TIMEOUT,USER_NOT_REGISTERED,NO_ROUTE_DESTINATION,NETWORK_OUT_OF_ORDER,RECOVERY_ON_TIMER_EXPIRE,NORMAL_TEMPORARY_FAILURE,ORIGINATOR_CANCEL"/>
      <action application="bridge" data="user/${destination_number}@$${domain}"/>

      <!-- 3. Ringback for the caller, then park until the sender moves us on. -->
      <action application="log" data="INFO push_wake: no progress from ${destination_number} (${originate_disposition}); parking for the push sender"/>
      <action application="ring_ready"/>
      <action application="sched_transfer" data="+15 ${destination_number} XML coralx-resume"/>
      <action application="park"/>
    </condition>
  </extension>

</include>
